  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why Indexes Exist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What An Index Is",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Separate, ordered, redundant structure beside the table",
            "Maps column values to row locations",
            "DB maintains it automatically — you never write to it",
            "Primary keys get one free; UNIQUE is a unique index"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Life Without One"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only access path is full table scan",
            "Read every page, discard non-matching rows",
            "O(N) work no matter how few rows match",
            "Heap rows sit in insertion order, not value order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Nothing about the heap helps you find WHERE email = 'x'. You look at all of it, every time."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- 1M users, heap = 8334 pages",
            "CREATE INDEX idx_users_email",
            "  ON users (email);  -- now ~3 reads"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Trade You Make",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Buy read speed with write cost and disk space",
            "Every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE also updates the index",
            "Index on a hot column taxes every write forever"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Selectivity & Cardinality"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Term",
            "Means",
            "Verdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Selectivity",
              "fraction matched",
              "1 of 1M = great"
            ],
            [
              "Low selectivity",
              "40% of table",
              "index loses"
            ],
            [
              "Cardinality",
              "distinct values",
              "user_id: high"
            ],
            [
              "is_deleted",
              "cardinality 2",
              "dead weight"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What To Index"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Columns in WHERE, JOIN ON, ORDER BY, GROUP BY",
            "Not columns you only SELECT",
            "Match the query's shape, not the table's schema"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "No Guarantees",
          "text": "The planner picks at runtime from statistics. An index that is used today can be ignored tomorrow as data grows."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Into The B+tree"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Internal nodes: separator keys + child pointers only",
            "All real keys live in leaf nodes",
            "Leaves are a doubly-linked list — walk sideways",
            "Range scans and ORDER BY get cheap, no re-descent",
            "Node = one disk page: 8KB Postgres, 16KB InnoDB"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Why Indexes Exist (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shallow By Design",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One node fetch = one I/O, so depth is everything",
            "High fan-out: hundreds of keys per node",
            "1M rows ≈ 3 levels; 1B rows ≈ 4-5 levels",
            "Cost is O(log_f N) page reads, f = fan-out",
            "Near-constant in practice — upper levels stay in RAM"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Staying Balanced"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Insert into a full node splits it in two",
            "A separator key is pushed up to the parent",
            "Deletes may merge sibling nodes back together"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What A Leaf Entry Holds"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Engine",
            "Leaf points to",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Postgres",
              "heap TID",
              "direct heap hit"
            ],
            [
              "InnoDB PK",
              "the row itself",
              "clustered"
            ],
            [
              "InnoDB 2nd",
              "PK value",
              "extra traversal"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Clustered index = table data stored in leaves, key order",
            "Only one clustered index per table (InnoDB, SQL Server)",
            "Secondary lookup = index hit, then PK tree walk",
            "So: keep primary keys narrow"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Order Buys You",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Works",
            "Does not"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "= < > BETWEEN IN",
              "LIKE '%suffix'"
            ],
            [
              "LIKE 'prefix%'",
              "lower(col) = ?"
            ],
            [
              "ORDER BY, MIN/MAX",
              "arbitrary functions"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Index Bloat"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Deleted entries leave dead space in leaves",
            "Postgres reclaims via VACUUM, or rebuild with REINDEX"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When B-tree Isn't Enough"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Hash: O(1) equality, no ranges, no order. Rarely worth it",
            "GIN: inverted — arrays, JSONB containment, full-text",
            "GIN makes one entry per element, not per row",
            "GiST: geometric, nearest-neighbour, range operators",
            "BRIN: min/max per block range, tiny on disk"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "BRIN's One Condition",
          "text": "Only pays off when physical row order correlates with the column — append-only timestamps are the classic fit."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bitmap Indexes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Oracle and analytics engines, low-cardinality columns",
            "Excellent in OLAP scans",
            "Terrible under concurrent writes — lock granularity"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Partial Index",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE INDEX idx_jobs_pending",
            "  ON jobs (created_at)",
            "  WHERE status = 'pending';",
            "-- indexes 0.4% of rows, not 100%"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "B-tree Internals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Expression Index"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- WHERE lower(email) = ? ignores (email)",
            "CREATE INDEX ON users (lower(email));",
            "-- expression must match the query exactly"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Case-Insensitive Search",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Case-folded search needs a matching expression index",
            "Or a citext / collation-aware column instead",
            "Mismatch falls back to a full scan, silently"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Unique Index"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Enforces the constraint and speeds lookups at once",
            "NULLs count as distinct in most engines",
            "So multiple NULLs pass a UNIQUE column"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Choose By Shape"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Best at"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "B-tree",
              "ordered, general, ~all queries"
            ],
            [
              "Hash",
              "equality only"
            ],
            [
              "GIN",
              "containment, text"
            ],
            [
              "GiST",
              "geometry, ranges"
            ],
            [
              "BRIN",
              "huge correlated tables"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Choosing Wrong",
          "text": "A GIN index on a plain integer column is slower and larger than the B-tree it replaced. Exotic ≠ faster."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Weigh It On Disk"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Size it: pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('idx'))",
            "Weigh that against your buffer pool budget"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Composite = Phone Book",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sorts by column 1, breaks ties with 2, then 3",
            "Exactly a book sorted by (last_name, first_name)",
            "You can find Smith, then Smith/Ada — not all Adas"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Leftmost Prefix Rule",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Index (a, b, c) serves: WHERE a",
            "Also: WHERE a AND b, and WHERE a AND b AND c",
            "Not: WHERE b alone. Not: WHERE c alone"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Equality Before Range"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- WHERE status='x' AND created_at > ?",
            "CREATE INDEX ON t (status, created_at);",
            "-- (created_at, status): scan then filter"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Once the index hits a range, seeking stops",
            "Later columns only filter or cover, never seek",
            "(tenant_id, created_at) makes a feed query a bounded scan",
            "That query sorts DESC LIMIT 20 with zero sort node"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Picking A Type, Then Ordering It",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Prefix Rule, Query By Query"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Query shape",
            "Index (a, b, c)"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "WHERE a",
              "seeks"
            ],
            [
              "WHERE a AND b",
              "seeks both"
            ],
            [
              "WHERE b",
              "no help"
            ],
            [
              "ORDER BY a, b",
              "order is free"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Redundancy Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "(a, b) makes a standalone (a) redundant — drop it",
            "(a, b) does NOT replace an index on (b)",
            "Need both directions? You need both indexes",
            "(a ASC, b DESC) serves that order and its exact reverse"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Ways To Lose It",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "WHERE b = ? on (a, b): full index scan at best",
            "ORDER BY a ASC, b ASC against (a ASC, b DESC)",
            "A sort the index can't supply brings back a Sort node"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Composite Myths",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cardinality-first ordering is folklore, not a rule",
            "Match the equality/range/sort shape instead",
            "More columns = wider entries = fewer keys per page",
            "Wider entries mean a deeper tree, bigger cache footprint",
            "Keep composites to exactly what queries need"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "IN Acts Like Equality"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- index (tenant_id, created_at)",
            "WHERE tenant_id IN (7, 9)",
            "  AND created_at > now() - interval '1 day'",
            "-- created_at can still be seeked"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Covering Index",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Index holds every column the query touches",
            "Engine never reads the heap or table at all",
            "All the work happens in the index leaves"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Names By Engine"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Engine",
            "What it's called"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Postgres",
              "Index Only Scan"
            ],
            [
              "SQL Server",
              "covered query"
            ],
            [
              "MySQL",
              "Extra: Using index"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "INCLUDE Payload"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE INDEX idx_ord_cust",
            "  ON orders (customer_id)",
            "  INCLUDE (total, status);",
            "-- payload in leaf, not in the sort key"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "INCLUDE columns cannot seek and cannot order",
            "They only satisfy the SELECT list",
            "That keeps the key narrow and the tree shallow"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Covering Indexes & Index-Only Scans",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Visibility Map Catch"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Postgres index-only scans still check the visibility map",
            "Pages not all-visible fall back to heap fetches",
            "EXPLAIN ANALYZE reports `Heap Fetches: N`",
            "Run VACUUM to refresh the map and skip the heap",
            "InnoDB: every secondary index covers the PK for free"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Win And The Price"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Best case: 5,000 random heap pages become one range read",
            "Cost: wider index and more write amplification",
            "Updating an included column now dirties the index too"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Anti-Pattern",
          "text": "SELECT * defeats covering by definition. Narrow the column list first, then consider covering."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Covered Or Not"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "SELECT list",
            "Covered?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "title",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "title, body",
              "no — heap"
            ],
            [
              "*",
              "never"
            ],
            [
              "count(*)",
              "yes"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Feed The Query From The Index",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "DROP INDEX idx_posts_user;",
            "CREATE INDEX ON posts (user_id, created_at)",
            "  INCLUDE (title);",
            "-- whole feed page served from leaves"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Prove It Covered",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT title",
            "  FROM posts WHERE user_id = 7;",
            "-- Index Only Scan, Heap Fetches: 0"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Compare buffer reads before and after, not plan shape",
            "Heap Fetches above 0 means VACUUM is overdue"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Build It To Shape",
          "text": "Equality columns first, then the one range column, then sort columns, then INCLUDE the payload."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When It's Worth It"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The query runs thousands of times a day",
            "Payload columns are rarely updated",
            "Random heap reads dominate the current plan"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cost-Based, Not Literal"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Planner enumerates candidate plans and costs each",
            "Picks the cheapest — ignores your written clause order",
            "Cost is an abstract unit built from I/O and CPU constants"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "How The Planner Decides",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Cost Constants"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Constant",
            "Default",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "seq_page_cost",
              "1.0",
              "baseline"
            ],
            [
              "random_page_cost",
              "4.0",
              "random read"
            ],
            [
              "cpu_tuple_cost",
              "0.01",
              "per row"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Statistics Drive Estimates"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Row counts, distinct values, MCV lists, histograms",
            "Collected by ANALYZE (Postgres) / ANALYZE TABLE (MySQL)",
            "Estimated rows × per-row cost = plan cost",
            "A bad row estimate is the #1 cause of a bad plan"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "After A Bulk Load",
          "text": "Stale stats say the table is tiny, so the planner picks a nested loop — over 10M rows. Run ANALYZE."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Correlation Breaks Estimates",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "WHERE city='Paris' AND country='France'",
            "-- est = frac(city) * frac(country)",
            "-- independence assumed -> wild underestimate",
            "CREATE STATISTICS s (dependencies)",
            "  ON city, country FROM addresses;"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Extended statistics teach the planner the dependency",
            "Or reshape the query to drop the redundant predicate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Tune For Your Disk"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "random_page_cost=4.0 assumes spinning platters",
            "On SSDs lower it to ~1.1",
            "Index scans then become correctly attractive"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Joins, Hints, Sniffing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Join order and method chosen with access paths together",
            "Indexes change which joins are even viable",
            "Hints: Oracle, MySQL FORCE INDEX, pg_hint_plan",
            "Prefer fixing statistics over pinning a plan",
            "Cached plans reuse a shape built for one odd parameter"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "EXPLAIN vs EXPLAIN ANALYZE",
          "text": "EXPLAIN shows the estimated plan only. EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the query and reports real timings and row counts."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Run It Safely",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "EXPLAIN ANALYZE DELETE FROM t WHERE id < 9;",
            "ROLLBACK;  -- ANALYZE really runs the DML"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Read Inside-Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Most indented nodes run first",
            "Each node feeds its parent upward",
            "So read bottom-up, deepest first"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "How The Planner Decides (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Node Line Anatomy",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "Seq Scan on orders  (cost=0.00..18334.00",
            "   rows=1000000 width=44)",
            "-- startup..total | est rows | avg bytes"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Startup cost = work before the first row emerges",
            "0 for a scan; high for a Sort or Hash build",
            "That is why LIMIT favours low-startup plans"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What ANALYZE Adds"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "(actual time=0.021..3.140 rows=998",
            "   loops=12)  -- time is PER loop",
            "-- real cost = 3.140 * 12 = 37.7ms"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "rows=1 vs rows=90000 is the smoking gun",
            "That gap means stale or missing statistics",
            "`Rows Removed by Filter: N` = read then thrown away",
            "Which means a missing or wrongly-ordered index"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Buffers And Formats"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) shows shared hits vs reads",
            "That is the real I/O story cost numbers approximate",
            "EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON) gives plan diffing in CI"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "MySQL Dialect"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Column",
            "Watch for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "type",
              "ALL = full scan"
            ],
            [
              "key, rows",
              "index chosen, est rows"
            ],
            [
              "Extra",
              "Using index (good)"
            ],
            [
              "Extra",
              "filesort, temporary"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Scan Nodes",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Node",
            "Does what"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Seq Scan",
              "all pages, sequential"
            ],
            [
              "Index Scan",
              "descend, then heap per row"
            ],
            [
              "Index Only Scan",
              "leaves only, Heap Fetches: 0"
            ],
            [
              "Bitmap Index Scan",
              "collect TIDs first"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "MySQL has real EXPLAIN ANALYZE since 8.0; before that only estimates."
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Reading EXPLAIN Output",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Seq Scan is the right call for large result fractions",
            "Index Scan pays random I/O, one heap visit per row"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bitmaps Combine Indexes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bitmap Heap Scan sorts TIDs by physical page",
            "Reads the heap once per page, not once per row",
            "BitmapAnd / BitmapOr merge two separate indexes",
            "That is how single-column indexes cooperate",
            "`Recheck Cond` is normal; lossy bitmaps re-test per row"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Join Methods"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Join",
            "Needs",
            "Good when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Nested Loop",
              "inner index",
              "outer side tiny"
            ],
            [
              "Hash Join",
              "memory",
              "no index, big inputs"
            ],
            [
              "Merge Join",
              "sorted inputs",
              "indexes supply order"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Nested loop is catastrophic when the outer side is large",
            "Hash join builds on the smaller input, probes with larger",
            "Merge join is free when the order already exists"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Memory Spills",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`Batches: 4` on a Hash Join = spilling to disk",
            "`Sort Method: external merge Disk: 24MB` = work_mem exceeded",
            "Raise work_mem, or supply an index that provides the order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Parallel Nodes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Gather and Parallel Seq Scan mean workers are running",
            "A seq scan across 8 workers can genuinely beat an index"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sequential Beats Random"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Not 'scan all vs scan some' — sequential I/O vs random I/O",
            "Sequential reads are ~4-10x cheaper per page",
            "An index scan on 10% of rows may touch every heap page",
            "Randomly, one at a time — and lose to one sequential pass"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where The Crossover Sits"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Rows matched",
            "Usual winner"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "under ~5%",
              "index scan"
            ],
            [
              "~5-25%",
              "bitmap heap scan"
            ],
            [
              "over ~25%",
              "seq scan"
            ],
            [
              "few hundred rows",
              "seq scan always"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The exact crossover moves with row width, correlation, and storage medium."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Plan Node Zoo & The Real Trade-off",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Correlation & CLUSTER",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- pg_stats.correlation near 1.0 = clustered",
            "CLUSTER orders USING idx_orders_created;",
            "-- one-time, locking, decays on UPDATE"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Clustered rows mean an index scan reads adjacent pages",
            "Uncorrelated rows mean one random page per match"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "LIMIT Flips The Math",
          "text": "An ordered index scan stops after 20 rows. A seq scan plus sort must process the whole table first."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Seq vs Index, Side By Side",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Seq scan",
            "Index scan"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "sequential I/O",
              "random I/O"
            ],
            [
              "no index upkeep",
              "write cost"
            ],
            [
              "parallelizable",
              "provides order"
            ],
            [
              "cost ∝ table size",
              "cost ∝ rows matched"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Bitmap heap scan owns the middle by turning random heap access into ordered page access."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Diagnostic Toggle",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SET enable_seqscan = off;  -- session only",
            "EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT ...;  -- see the cost",
            "RESET enable_seqscan;  -- always reset"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Use it to confirm the planner was right, then stop",
            "Never leave enable_* toggles on in production",
            "They are a diagnostic, not a fix"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Find The Query First",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "pg_stat_statements ordered by total_exec_time",
            "MySQL: slow query log or performance_schema",
            "Optimize total time, not the worst single run",
            "5ms run 2M times beats a 3s hourly report",
            "Explain on production-shaped data, never a 1k-row dev table"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Write Tax"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "8 indexes = 9 structural updates per INSERT",
            "Plus WAL / redo written for every one of them",
            "HOT updates skip index work only if no indexed column changed",
            "And only if the new tuple fits on the same page",
            "More indexes = more HOT misses"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
